# Code owners for Cinzal
#
# Every pull request automatically requests review from the owners of the
# paths it touches. Rules are last-match-wins, so the catch-all comes first
# and the specific cases below it.
#
# The specific rules are redundant today — every path resolves to the same
# owner. They are here as documentation: this is the list of places where a
# mistake is expensive, and it should stay accurate as contributors arrive.
# See docs/project/cinzal-implementation-plan.md for what each one protects.

# Default owner for everything in the repository.
*                                   @garnizeh

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The fog boundary — the game's core security property (RFC-001 §3, §9).
#
# The client must never hold the full match state. All state reaching a player
# passes through Project(). A change here can leak hidden information without
# breaking a single test that was not written to catch it, and the fog suite
# is deliberately negative: it asserts hidden facts are ABSENT.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/internal/rules/                    @garnizeh
/internal/view/                     @garnizeh
/internal/render/                   @garnizeh

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The enforcement machinery (RFC-001 §5, §15.1).
#
# CI is what makes the fog boundary and the purity of `rules` real rather than
# conventional: the import checks, the debug-tag isolation, the determinism
# assertions. Weakening a gate here silently weakens every guarantee that
# depends on it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/.github/                           @garnizeh
/Makefile                           @garnizeh

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The specifications.
#
# The GDD is authoritative on rules, the RFC on architecture. Both are
# changelogged, and entries record why a decision was made — which is the part
# that matters when someone revisits it. Changes to game rules or architecture
# belong here first and in code second.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/docs/project/                      @garnizeh

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database schema and migrations.
#
# Migrations run at process start behind an advisory lock and are effectively
# irreversible once deployed. The order log is irreplaceable: there is no state
# table to fall back on (RFC-001 §18).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/internal/store/migrations/         @garnizeh
